WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.660
Is our cloud sovereign?

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:03.800
There is no yes or no answer.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.280
There is a level for
each of four questions.

00:00:07.683 --> 00:00:08.933
First, which level.

00:00:09.288 --> 00:00:13.448
The European Commission's Cloud
Sovereignty Framework splits sovereign

00:00:13.448 --> 00:00:14.918
into eight objectives.

00:00:15.428 --> 00:00:21.418
A provider scores from SEAL-0 to SEAL-4,
and the lowest objective sets the level.

00:00:21.633 --> 00:00:25.843
For its own tender, the Commission
asks for SEAL-2, data sovereignty.

00:00:26.348 --> 00:00:29.078
The framework is a
procurement tool, not a law.

00:00:30.117 --> 00:00:33.747
Second, jurisdiction follows the company,
not the server.

00:00:34.097 --> 00:00:39.167
The US CLOUD Act requires a US provider
to hand over data in its control,

00:00:39.597 --> 00:00:42.367
inside or outside the United States.

00:00:42.782 --> 00:00:48.062
GDPR Article 48 says a foreign
order is enforceable in the EU

00:00:48.062 --> 00:00:51.212
only if an international
agreement backs it.

00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:53.437
The provider sits between two laws.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:58.260
Third, European infrastructure
with SaaS outside it.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.840
The SaaS sets our level,
because that is where people work.

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:05.365
Content, identities and
metadata usually leave.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.930
Support access and AI processing

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.610
leave under conditions we can negotiate.

00:01:11.115 --> 00:01:12.745
Keys we hold stay with us.

00:01:13.783 --> 00:01:14.993
Fourth, could we leave?

00:01:15.368 --> 00:01:19.618
If a sanction, a court order or a
bankruptcy ended the service tomorrow,

00:01:20.108 --> 00:01:21.698
how long until we work again?

00:01:21.938 --> 00:01:26.068
The EU Data Act, a law,
caps the switching notice at two months.

00:01:26.513 --> 00:01:30.883
DORA requires exit strategies
for critical services in finance.

00:01:31.258 --> 00:01:35.868
The simple test is to export everything,
open it elsewhere, and time it.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.060
What good looks like.

00:01:38.345 --> 00:01:41.075
Score suppliers on the
framework's eight objectives.

00:01:41.605 --> 00:01:43.035
Hold the keys ourselves.

00:01:43.385 --> 00:01:45.675
Map every flow that leaves each SaaS.

00:01:45.955 --> 00:01:49.045
Test one exit a year for
each critical service.

00:01:50.897 --> 00:01:52.107
A suggested first step.

00:01:52.392 --> 00:01:53.722
Decide three things.

00:01:53.987 --> 00:01:57.317
Which data must never be
reachable by a foreign order.

00:01:57.642 --> 00:01:59.532
Which level we accept for the rest.

00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:02.687
And a tested exit for
every critical service.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.800
Sovereign is a level, not a yes or no.

00:02:07.075 --> 00:02:08.925
Ask which level, for which data.

